<|SECTION:TASK|>
Analyze the finalized passage below. Do not continue the story. For each named character, work in two ordered steps: first identify inaccessible information, then write the accessible passage using the bounded rewrite regions below. The audience selects accessible information, not a new narrator. Do not add speaker labels.

STEP 1, EXCLUSIONS: List each part this character cannot perceive as an exact unique span copied character for character from the passage, with a one-word reason such as inaudible, private-thought, unseen, asleep, absent or reader-only. Exclude only inaccessible information; retain every accessible fact and event, including observable acts mixed with private content. For each exclusion, supply a "forbidden" array naming only the secret-bearing words or phrases that must not return in this audience's rewritten text: concealed objects, identities, names, acts or private content. Use [] if there are none. Do not ban ordinary grammatical words or facts the audience can perceive. The host checks those words and phrases case-insensitively at word boundaries. The list is not permission to disclose an unlisted secret. Use exclusions [] when everything is accessible. If nothing is accessible, set status omitted, exclusions [] and text null.{% if rewrite_hidden_traces %}

OBSERVABLE TRACES: For each exclusion, preserve any supported effect this audience could perceive through sound, touch, smell, vibration or a visible side effect. Put that ambiguous sensory rendering in an optional "trace" string. Name only the effect, never the hidden object, act, purpose or private content. Do not retain the narrator as the acting cause when the audience only senses an unidentified effect: felt pressure is not knowledge of who moved what. An outward performance remains perceptible even when its sincerity is hidden: feigned sleep becomes an uncertain appearance of sleep, not merely closed eyes, actual sleep, or disclosure of pretending. An out-of-earshot exchange keeps its visible quiet exchange without the words or subject. Do not invent an effect merely because it is plausible. Silent unobserved acts with no supported sensory effect stay deleted. An unconscious or absent audience receives no unsupported trace. Keep the original voice, person and tense. Step 2 may reword a trace within its rewrite region, but must retain its sensory fact and uncertainty without duplicating an effect already present.{% endif %}

STEP 2, TEXT: Each sentence touched by an exclusion{% if rewrite_hidden_traces %} or its trace{% endif %}, together with its immediately preceding and following sentences, forms a rewrite region. Merge overlapping regions; do not expand again from a neighbour. Sentence-ending punctuation and standalone lines or headings delimit these sentences. Inside a rewrite region, write good, grammatically correct prose, favouring prose quality over verbatim wording. Preserve every accessible fact, event, attribution and uncertainty. Keep the passage's voice, tense, narrator and grammatical person; the original speaker still says "I". Remove inaccessible information{% if rewrite_hidden_traces %} while preserving the declared sensory effects{% endif %}. Neither an excluded span nor a forbidden word or phrase may reappear in a rewritten region. Repair broken joins, fragments and punctuation. If only part of a sentence is excluded, its region must not become empty. Neighbouring sentences may be reworded for a smooth join, but their facts and events must survive. Do not invent a new fact, observation, action or consequence.

Outside those regions, copy every untouched sentence byte for byte, in order, with no additions, omissions or changes. With no exclusions, copy the entire passage exactly. Do not summarize or add perception-report framing such as "Bob sees". The host checks region bounds, unchanged text, excluded spans, forbidden words/phrases and nonempty partial-sentence regions. It cannot decide whether your rewritten prose preserves the facts; you must check that yourself before returning it.


Use only supported events. Prior physical context is author evidence, not knowledge shared by the cast. Established knowledge belongs only to its named character. Authored descriptions and details are background, not proof of witnessing a new event. Preserve attribution, uncertainty and established beliefs: hearing a claim does not make it true. Do not invent beliefs, observations, disclosure, an arrival, awakening, or an opportunity to overhear. You have no authority to activate or deactivate characters.

Presence is a contextual hint, never proof of attention, consciousness, audibility or access to private thought. A speaker knows their own stated private thought; other characters do not gain it from adjacent public speech. Whispered words reach only supported recipients. An unconscious bystander does not witness a passage merely because they are listed as present. A named but absent character gets an omission unless the supplied events explicitly support perception. Unknown presence is not permission to invent access.

Assess each audience separately. A restriction scoped to one observer, such as their turned back or distance, does not conceal the same event from its awake participants. A direct transfer to an awake recipient remains accessible unless the passage or context establishes concealment from that recipient; secrecy from a bystander alone does not establish that. Conversely, feeling contact does not identify its actor, object or hidden manipulation when the scene explicitly blocks that identification. Exclude those causes, even if a tactile effect remains. Preserve public surroundings and physical properties embedded in a sentence about a hidden observer; being beside a secret in the prose does not make those facts secret.

Tracked states carry each character's latest recorded state while it remains tracked, including states outside the recent passage window. Their time prefixes give the record age, not an expiry. Account for impairments such as sleep, intoxication or a blindfold when assessing perception. These are author-maintained states, not knowledge automatically shared with other characters.

The passage sub_type identifies the reader operation (query, visual-scene, visual-character, or examine); subject_character is the character being described, not an initiating investigator. These fields do not establish an in-world action. Reinforcements are author-maintained state, not speech or a discovery event. Investigations may reveal information only to the reader: a query, visual description or examination does not by itself establish that a character investigated or learned its answer. Only a supported in-world action or established knowledge can justify a character's view. Omit reader-only findings when no such evidence exists. Titles and questions are part of the supplied passage: exclude secret-bearing headings as well as answers. A character named in a heading is not automatically its audience.

Each ready view must contain everything this character can perceive of the current passage, without revealing inaccessible content or naming withheld secrets. Do not output the full reader passage with a private annotation. Do not restate unrelated earlier events as new learning. Leave the reader's prose untouched.

OBSERVABLE FACT CHECK: Resolve physical referents before excluding private mental content. If the supplied physical context shows a character directly perceiving or participating in the same recognizable activity named in a thought, KEEP ITS SPECIFIC NAME in an explicit physical clause in that character's view, even if only the thought names it. Keep the adjacent speech too. For example, with two awake people playing at the same board, "I loved playing chess with her. 'Your turn,' I said." becomes "I was playing chess with her. 'Your turn,' I said." for the other player. Keep the original speaker as "I"; exclude only the private love. Follow the actual passage's writing, not the tense or person of this example. For objects, keep a specific identity only if visible identifying features or the viewer's established knowledge support it. Seeing a plain vase does not reveal that it is a stolen Ming piece: keep "vase", not the hidden identity or provenance. Shared presence alone is insufficient. Concealed objects, unobserved acts, feelings, intentions and memories remain private; thinking about a hidden key does not expose it. This retains an observable situation and invents no discovery.

Return ONLY a JSON object with a views array. Exactly one entry per character in cast, using their exact name. Fields, in this order: audience (one-element array), status (ready or omitted), exclusions (array of {"span": exact passage text, "reason": one word, "forbidden": array of secret-bearing words or phrases{% if rewrite_hidden_traces %}, "trace": optional sensory rendering{% endif %}}; [] when nothing is excluded or the view is omitted), text (the accessible passage with bounded regions rewritten for ready, null for omitted), knowledge_ids (array of supplied established-knowledge IDs actually used for this view), passage_ids (array of prior physical-context message IDs actually used for this view). Both ID arrays are required; use [] when there are no citations for that array. Cite only decisive inputs, not every available context item. For an omission caused by an earlier passage (for example, unconsciousness), cite that passage even though the character did not perceive it. Do not include the current passage ID in passage_ids: the host always records it itself, and ignores any citation of it. Never invent IDs or cite another audience's knowledge. No other fields, explanation or surrounding prose.
Example shape: {"views": [{"audience": ["Alice"], "status": "ready", "exclusions": [], "text": "<the passage, verbatim>", "knowledge_ids": [], "passage_ids": []}, {"audience": ["Bob"], "status": "ready", "exclusions": [{"span": "<exact passage text>", "reason": "inaudible", "forbidden": ["<secret-bearing phrase>"]}], "text": "<the accessible passage with bounded rewrites>", "knowledge_ids": [], "passage_ids": []}, {"audience": ["Cora"], "status": "omitted", "exclusions": [], "text": null, "knowledge_ids": [], "passage_ids": [12]}]}
